# Teensy test
# A manual test, and the instrument is your eye. Nothing on the installation moves,
# lights or sounds: a Teensy 4.1 on its adapter plate on the backplane, a USB lead, and
# nothing else mounted.
# Sketch: Source code/Arduino/teensy_check/. Pick the Teensy under 'com port' and press
# 'flash firmware' first; the run itself is one pass of about a second.

0 Nothing on the piece moves, lights or sounds. No servo is commanded, no NeoPixel changes, no body sings.
0 The chosen lead is opened. A Teensy does not reboot when its port opens, so nothing greets: the board is asked for its status line instead, and the page shows it verbatim. If nothing answers, the run stops there and says to flash it.
0 The status line is judged: the sketch's name and version, the board, the processor clock (600 MHz by default) and the die temperature from the processor's own sensor.
0 A hundred lines are sent and echoed back over USB, each one random. Every one has to come back exactly, and the mean and worst round trip are shown.
0 The status is asked again, and the board's uptime between the two answers is compared with this computer's clock. They have to agree to within a few hundredths.
0 The Teensy's orange LED, which has been blinking slowly all along, flickers fast for five seconds.
0 **Look at it.** That is the half no reading can do. Press 'the LED blinked' or 'the LED did not blink', and the page keeps what you said beside the checks.
5 The LED goes back to its slow blink. The run is over; the port is closed and the Teensy keeps running the sketch.
